CHUNK({"chunk":"4f","module":"m4","topics":[{"id":"m4-energy-basics","title":"Energy conversion and fuels","short":"Energy conversion","blurb":"The law of conservation of energy, the forms of energy that power plants convert, and the fossil fuel families.","source":"Lecture 16, pages 1–7; Lecture 17, pages 1–3","tabs":[{"title":"Conservation of energy","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Energy cannot be created or destroyed. It can only be <b>converted from one form to another</b>, so the total energy in a closed system stays constant.</p>"},{"title":"Forms and sources","html":"<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Sources</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Mechanical energy</td><td>Hydropower, wind power, tidal power</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thermal energy</td><td>Fossil fuel power plants (coal, natural gas, oil), geothermal energy, nuclear power plants</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Radiant energy</td><td>Solar panels (PV cells)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chemical energy</td><td>Batteries, fuel cells (like hydrogen fuel cells)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Fossil fuels come as <b>solid</b> (wood, coal), <b>liquid</b> (petrol, diesel, paraffin, crude oil) and <b>gaseous</b> (natural gas).</p>"},{"title":"Renewable or not","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Non-renewable</h4><p>Coal, oil, natural gas, nuclear fuels.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Renewable</h4><p>Sun, wind, hydroelectric power.</p></div></div>\n<p><b>Sustainable and renewable energy technologies</b> aim to cut environmental impact, reduce dependence on fossil fuels and secure long-term energy supply. The technologies are wind, solar, hydropower, biomass and bioenergy, and geothermal.</p>\n<p>The criteria for a sustainable technology:</p>\n<ul><li>Low or zero greenhouse gas emissions.</li><li>Minimal environmental degradation.</li><li>Efficient land and water use.</li><li>Safety and long-term viability.</li></ul>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"State the law of conservation of energy.","back":"Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only converted from one form to another; the total in a closed system stays constant."},{"front":"Which energy form do hydro, wind and tidal power use?","back":"Mechanical energy."},{"front":"Which energy forms do batteries and fuel cells store?","back":"Chemical energy."}],"cardCount":5,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-steam-plant","title":"Steam (thermal) power plant","short":"Steam power plant","blurb":"How fuel becomes electricity in a steam plant, and its advantages and disadvantages.","source":"Lecture 16, pages 8–12","tabs":[{"title":"Energy chain","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A steam power plant converts the chemical energy of fuel into electricity through steam.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m4-steam-chain\"></figure>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Fuel</b> The primary energy source.</li><li><b>Superheated steam</b> Heat energy.</li><li><b>Turbine shaft rotation</b> Kinetic energy.</li><li><b>Generator</b> Electrical energy.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Pros and cons","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Initial cost and construction time are <b>less</b> than for a hydroelectric plant.</li><li>Fuel transport cost is less if the plant is near the load centre.</li></ul></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Disadvantages</h4><ul><li>Non-renewable source of energy.</li><li>Cannot be used as a peak load plant.</li><li>Cost of generation is considerably higher than hydroelectric.</li><li>Atmospheric contamination.</li><li>High turbine rpm needs special materials and fabrication methods.</li></ul></div></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Give the energy chain in a steam power plant.","back":"Fuel → superheated steam (heat) → turbine shaft rotation (kinetic) → generator (electrical)."},{"front":"Name two advantages of a thermal plant over hydro.","back":"Lower initial cost and shorter construction time; lower fuel transport cost when near the load centre."},{"front":"Name three disadvantages of a thermal plant.","back":"Non-renewable, not suitable as a peak load plant, high generation cost, atmospheric contamination, turbine needs special materials."}],"cardCount":3,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-nuclear","title":"Nuclear power plant","short":"Nuclear power plant","blurb":"Fission, the parts of a reactor, the two plant types and the pros and cons.","source":"Lecture 16, pages 13–18","tabs":[{"title":"Fission","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Nuclear fission</b>: when the nucleus of a uranium atom is split, some neutrons escape and hit other uranium atoms, making them split too. This is the <b>chain reaction</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>1 kg of uranium</span><b>Complete fission releases the energy of 4500 tonnes of coal or 2000 tonnes of oil</b></div><div><span>1 metric tonne of uranium</span><b>= 3 million metric tons of coal = 12 million barrels of oil</b></div></div>"},{"title":"Reactor parts","html":"<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Fuel</span><b>U235 isotope</b></div><div><span>Core</span><b>The chamber where fission takes place</b></div><div><span>Moderator</span><b>Slows fast neutrons. Graphite, water or heavy water</b></div><div><span>Control rods</span><b>Regulate the rate of the chain reaction. Made of boron or cadmium, which absorb neutrons</b></div><div><span>Cooling system</span><b>Removes the intense heat. Water or liquid sodium as coolant</b></div><div><span>Thermal shielding</span><b>Protects from radiation and radiation fragments</b></div></div>"},{"title":"Plant types","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Two types: the <b>pressurised water reactor (PWR)</b> and the <b>boiling water reactor (BWR)</b>. About 65–70% of the world's nuclear reactors are PWRs.</p>"},{"title":"Pros and cons","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Less space and less fuel consumption.</li><li>Not affected by weather.</li></ul></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Disadvantages</h4><ul><li>Higher building cost than thermal or hydro.</li><li>Radioactive waste must be disposed of carefully.</li><li>High maintenance cost.</li><li>Well-trained operators needed.</li></ul></div></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is nuclear fission?","back":"Splitting a uranium nucleus; escaping neutrons split other nuclei in a chain reaction."},{"front":"What is the fuel in a nuclear reactor?","back":"The U235 isotope."},{"front":"What does the moderator do and what is it made of?","back":"Slows fast neutrons; graphite, water or heavy water."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-hydro-wind","title":"Hydroelectric and wind power","short":"Hydro and wind","blurb":"Hydel plants and their turbines, and horizontal axis wind turbines.","source":"Lecture 17, pages 4–12","tabs":[{"title":"Hydroelectric","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A hydel plant converts the <b>potential energy of water</b> into the kinetic energy of a turbine, and then into electrical energy through a generator.</p>\n<ul><li>Renewable and the cheapest energy source.</li><li>Long life of <b>100–125 years</b>; very low maintenance and operation cost.</li><li>No air pollution and no fuel transport problem.</li><li>Also used for flood control, irrigation, fishery and recreation.</li><li>Turbines run at low speed, <b>300–400 rpm</b>, so no special materials are needed.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Turbine choice","html":"<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Plant</th><th>Turbine</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>High head</td><td>Pelton wheel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Medium head</td><td>Francis turbine</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Low head</td><td>Kaplan turbine</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"},{"title":"Wind turbine","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A wind turbine (aero generator) is an electric motor in reverse: wind kinetic energy in, electricity out.</p>\n<p>The blades capture the wind's kinetic energy and turn it into rotational energy, which goes through a series of <b>gears to raise the speed</b> of a generator.</p>\n<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Low cost power.</li><li>Less maintenance.</li><li>No fossil fuel.</li><li>Power goes straight to the grid.</li></ul></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Disadvantages</h4><ul><li>High initial fabrication cost.</li><li>Large land area.</li><li>Must shut down in severe weather.</li><li>Very noisy.</li></ul></div></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Energy chain in a hydel plant?","back":"Potential energy of water → kinetic energy of the turbine → electrical energy from the generator."},{"front":"Life and turbine speed of a hydro plant?","back":"100–125 years; 300–400 rpm."},{"front":"Which turbine for high, medium and low head?","back":"Pelton wheel, Francis turbine, Kaplan turbine."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-solar-biomass","title":"Solar, biomass and sustainability","short":"Solar and biomass","blurb":"Solar PV, concentrated solar power, solar thermal and biomass plants, and how plants compare for sustainability.","source":"Lecture 17, pages 13–24","tabs":[{"title":"Solar PV","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Solar photovoltaic (PV)</b> systems convert sunlight <b>directly into electricity</b> with solar panels.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Sunlight hits</b> the panel; photons hit silicon cells.</li><li><b>Photons transfer energy</b> to electrons, which become free and move.</li><li><b>Electric field</b> in the solar cell pushes electrons to the N-type layer.</li><li><b>Current flows</b> through the external circuit as DC electricity.</li><li><b>Inverter</b> turns DC into AC to power the home, business or load.</li></ol>"},{"title":"CSP and solar thermal","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Concentrated solar power</h4><p>Mirrors concentrate sunlight onto a receiver, which heats a working fluid (molten salt, oil or steam). A heat exchanger makes high-pressure steam, the steam drives a turbine and generator, and AC electricity goes to the grid or is used on site.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Solar thermal</h4><p>Collectors on a roof absorb heat and pass it to a fluid. The fluid circulates through pipes to a storage tank (for water heating), then the cooled fluid returns to the collector in a closed loop.</p></div></div>"},{"title":"Biomass","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <b>biomass plant</b> burns organic material (wood, crop waste) to make electricity or heat. It uses renewable organic material instead of coal or gas.</p>\n<p>Biomass is renewable only if it is sourced, harvested and regrown sustainably.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Biomass fuel</b> is the energy source.</li><li><b>Boiler</b> burns it to make heat.</li><li><b>Steam system</b> turns water into steam.</li><li><b>Turbine</b> is driven by steam.</li><li><b>Generator</b> converts motion to electricity.</li><li><b>Condenser</b> turns used steam back to water.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Sustainability","html":"<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Plant</th><th>Resource</th><th>Environmental impact</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Coal</td><td>Non-renewable</td><td>High CO<sub>2</sub>, SO<sub>2</sub>, NO<sub>x</sub> and particulates: air pollution and climate change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Natural gas</td><td>Non-renewable</td><td>Lower emissions than coal, but still CO<sub>2</sub> and methane leakage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nuclear</td><td>Non-renewable (uranium, thorium), long-lasting reserves</td><td>Very low CO<sub>2</sub> in operation; radioactive waste and safety concerns</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hydropower</td><td>Renewable (flowing water)</td><td>No direct CO<sub>2</sub>; large dams can disrupt ecosystems and displace communities</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Solar (PV and CSP)</td><td>Renewable (sunlight)</td><td>No emissions in operation; land use and rare metal sourcing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wind</td><td>Renewable (wind)</td><td>No CO<sub>2</sub>; some impact on birds, noise, view</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Biomass</td><td>Renewable (organic matter, waste)</td><td>CO<sub>2</sub> neutral if sustainable; combustion releases particulates and NO<sub>x</sub></td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Fossil fuel plants are unsustainable in the long run. Nuclear remains low-carbon but controversial. <b>Renewables (solar, wind, hydro) are the most sustainable.</b></p>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does a solar PV cell produce current?","back":"Photons free electrons in silicon; the cell's electric field pushes them to the N-type layer; they flow through the external circuit as DC."},{"front":"Why does a PV system need an inverter?","back":"To convert DC from the panels into AC for the load."},{"front":"How does CSP generate electricity?","back":"Mirrors focus sunlight on a receiver heating a fluid; a heat exchanger makes steam that drives a turbine and generator."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[{"id":"m4-steam-chain","src":"m4/pages/b28.png","crop":null,"caption":"Energy chain of a steam power plant: fuel, steam, turbine, generator.","alt":"Four boxes in a row: fuel, superheated steam, turbine shaft, generator."}]});
